    "body": "Sixth of the stack, on top of #2500. The signature's expectations now reach into sub-modules and destructuring patterns, so a problem is reported where it is.\n\n- **Sub-modules.** `module Inner = \u2026` items receive the sub-signature the outer signature declares for them. A member the sub-module lacks is `Module is missing members: y` on the inner literal; a differing type member lands on its `type` item; a wrong definition lands on the definition. Before, all three surfaced as one generic mismatch on the outer module. A sub-module the user annotated keeps its own annotation.\n- **Destructuring.** Variables inside tuple, labeled tuple, nested tuple, list and constructor patterns are annotated individually (`let (a, b) = \u2026` is checked as `(a : Int, b : Int)`), so `let (a, b) = (1, \"s\")` against `a : Int; b : Int` reports on `\"s\"`. The tuple literal is also marked, exactly as a top-level `let (a : Int, b : Int) = (1, \"s\")` is today; that cascade belongs to the generic expected/got reporting and is left for a later change.\n- The elaboration refold strips the synthetic ascriptions by walking the user's pattern and its elaboration side by side.\n\nSlide items 20a and 20b show both cases.\n\n## Verification\n\n`make test-quick` green \u00b7 the Modules slide analyzes with errors only under its \"Error\" items.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
